      <h2>What Metadata Do Android Phones (Samsung, Google Pixel, Xiaomi) Store in Photos?</h2>
      <p>
        Modern Android smartphones (including Samsung Galaxy, Google Pixel, Xiaomi, OnePlus, and Motorola devices) capture rich computational imagery. However, behind every high-resolution capture lies an intricate diagnostic and forensic audit trail embedded within the file binary:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Precise GPS Geotags:</strong> High-precision latitude, longitude, and elevation recorded via cellular tower triangulation, Wi-Fi BSSID scanning, and GNSS satellite positioning, pinpointing your exact location down to building floor level.</li>
        <li><strong>Hardware &amp; Software Identifiers:</strong> Manufacturer make and model (e.g. <code>Samsung Galaxy S24 Ultra</code> or <code>Google Pixel 8 Pro</code>), Android OS build fingerprint, camera sensor model, and optical stabilization telemetry.</li>
        <li><strong>OEM-Specific MakerNote Payloads:</strong> Proprietary binary structures containing Nightography HDR exposure fusion logs, Scene Optimizer AI classification labels (e.g. &quot;Food&quot;, &quot;Document&quot;, &quot;Pet&quot;), and facial contour coordinates.</li>
        <li><strong>Google Photos &amp; Motion Photo Metadata:</strong> Embedded XMP micro-video containers (<code>GCamera:MicroVideo</code>) that store secondary MP4 video clips directly inside what appears to be a static JPEG file.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        RemoveSynth strips all of these personal, diagnostic, and motion markers completely inside your phone's web browser before sharing.
      </p>

      <h2>Android Camera Architectures: Google Camera (GCam) vs Samsung One UI EXIF</h2>
      <p>
        Different Android phone manufacturers format image headers according to divergent software specifications:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Samsung Camera (One UI):</strong> Embeds custom Samsung MakerNote blocks in EXIF tag <code>0x927C</code>, alongside dual-camera depth maps and wide-angle barrel distortion compensation profiles.</li>
        <li><strong>Google Camera (Pixel):</strong> Heavily utilizes the Adobe XMP metadata standard to encapsulate Ultra HDR gain maps, computational blur depth layers, and Motion Photo MP4 clips directly inside multiple <code>APP1</code> marker segments.</li>
        <li><strong>Xiaomi and OnePlus:</strong> Frequently include custom Hasselblad or Leica color calibration metadata and watermarking timestamps.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Mathematical Formulation of Android JPEG APP1 and XMP Tag Stripping</h2>
      <p>
        Android photo files follow the JPEG/JFIF format (ISO/IEC 10918-1). The file begins with a 2-byte Start of Image (SOI) marker (<code>0xFFD8</code>), immediately followed by application marker segments:
      </p>
      <div class="math-block">
        <code>JPEG_Stream = SOI || Marker_Segment_1 || Marker_Segment_2 || ... || SOS || Entropy_Compressed_Pixels || EOI</code>
      </div>
      <p>
        Each application segment carries a 2-byte identifier and a 16-bit big-endian payload length:
      </p>
      <div class="math-block">
        <code>APP1_Segment = ( 0xFFE1, Length_16, Header_Namespace, TIFF_Payload )</code>
      </div>
      <p>
        To permanently strip location, camera hardware tags, and proprietary AI classifications, our sanitization engine scans the stream and excises both the EXIF APP1 segment (<code>Header == 'Exif\0\0'</code>) and all Google Camera XMP segments (<code>Header == 'http://ns.adobe.com/xap/1.0/\0'</code>):
      </p>
      <div class="math-block">
        <code>Sanitized_JPEG = JPEG_Stream \ &#123; Segment | Segment.Type == 0xFFE1 &#125;</code>
      </div>
      <p>
        Because the Start of Scan (SOS) and compressed 8x8 DCT pixel blocks remain bit-for-bit untouched, image visual fidelity remains mathematically lossless ($PSNR = \infty$).
      </p>

      <h2>Worked Example: Sanitizing a 14.8MB Samsung Galaxy S24 Ultra Capture</h2>
      <p>
        To observe the performance of our in-browser cleaner on Android hardware, consider this empirical benchmark:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Test Device:</strong> Samsung Galaxy S24 Ultra running Android 14 (One UI 6.1)</li>
        <li><strong>Camera Capture:</strong> 200-Megapixel primary sensor shot in High Resolution mode (<code>20261024_142018.jpg</code>)</li>
        <li><strong>Original File Size:</strong> 15,518,400 bytes (14.80 Megabytes)</li>
        <li><strong>Resolution:</strong> 16320 by 12240 pixels</li>
        <li><strong>Detected Sensitive Metadata:</strong> GPS Latitude (<code>34 deg 03 min 12.4 sec N</code>), GPS Longitude (<code>118 deg 14 min 36.8 sec W</code>), Altitude (78.4m), Device Model (<code>SM-S928B</code>), Samsung Scene Optimizer Tag (&quot;Cityscape Night&quot;), Motion Photo payload (2.8 MB embedded MP4)</li>
        <li><strong>RemoveSynth In-Browser Scrubbing:</strong> Excised EXIF APP1, wiped GPS coordinates, stripped Samsung MakerNote and embedded Motion Photo video stream</li>
        <li><strong>Clean Output Size:</strong> 12,680,200 bytes (12.09 Megabytes)</li>
        <li><strong>Byte Reduction:</strong> 2.71 Megabytes of hidden tracking and auxiliary bloat eliminated</li>
        <li><strong>Visual Quality:</strong> 100 percent identical compressed pixel buffers</li>
        <li><strong>Processing Runtime:</strong> 36 milliseconds in Google Chrome mobile V8 engine</li>
      </ul>

